  "worked_solution": "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (1+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} & ={e}^{1/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}=x.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
